# Mac observer and supervisor evidence protocol — HOLD

Source candidate only; no native compilation, execution or installation evidence.
**No native path in this package produces `stopped:true`.** A process-only seal is
diagnostic evidence and cannot release a host slot or authorize publication.

## Implemented source and missing boundary

`native/mac-es-observer.c` contains an explicit, opt-in public Endpoint Security
observer. It creates a real ES client and subscribes to `NOTIFY_FORK`, `NOTIFY_EXEC`
and `NOTIFY_EXIT`. It records full audit-token words, process start time, message
version, global/per-type sequence counters and Mach time. It does not filter by
account. Version <5, dropped/reordered/wrapped counters, unsupported events, malformed
identities, bounded-journal exhaustion and write/fsync failure poison observation.
The first event of each type establishes its baseline; it says nothing about an
earlier interval. No readiness, launch binding, final seal or stop proof is emitted.

This is a C library with no executable main, installer, entitlement changes, build
script, service definition, network listener or automatic invocation. The privileged
caller must provide a fresh exclusive file descriptor under a protected directory.
Only numeric process metadata is written; no executable paths, argv, environment,
source, output or credentials. Its unsigned raw stream is **not accepted** as the
TypeScript authenticated journal. It is collection source, not a finished supervisor.
Synchronous fsync can cause backpressure and event drops; loss invalidates coverage.
Shutdown errors retain poisoned state rather than freeing possible callback state.

`src/mac-attestation.ts` implements a concrete pinned local supervisor client and a
bounded signed-journal reader/replayer. `NativeDescendantAttestor` is now a class,
not an injectable `inspect => stopped` interface. `NativeMacAdapter` accepts that
class or no observer. The old `ps` scan and arbitrary success callback are removed.
Without an observer the existing group launch/stop remains best effort and unknown.
With an observer, explicit start requires an already provisioned compatible client;
absence, bad pins, missing evidence or ambiguity fails closed. No companion binary,
IPC implementation or signing service is supplied or claimed to exist.

## Owner-only configuration and launch boundary

`MacSupervisorConfig` contains canonical `clientExecutable`, its SHA-256,
`supervisorSha256`, protected root-owned `evidenceRoot`, and an Ed25519 **public** PEM.
Constructors perform no filesystem/process/provider I/O. Only explicit methods do.
Public-key configuration and binary hashes are trust pins, not measured proof of a
live entitlement, ES subscription, helper installation or current authority.

The client executable and every path ancestor must be root-owned without group or
world write permission. Setuid/setgid executables and linked files are rejected.
The executable is hashed before control invocation (16 MiB ceiling). The supervisor
must authenticate the caller's kernel audit token through IPC and the client must
authenticate the privileged peer/code requirement. Tickets never replace authority.
Root-owned evidence files permit group read but no world access; protected ancestry,
ACLs and a local durable filesystem remain host prerequisites.

`start(plan, recordIdentity, beforeStart)` accepts only a branded, owner-approved
trusted Xcode plan. It creates a random 256-bit correlation ticket, persists its
identity and intent marker, then invokes these fixed client operations without a shell:

- `reserve --protocol foundry-mac-es-journal-v1 --ticket T --attempt-hash A
  --profile-hash P --intent-hash I --uid U --gid G`
- `run --protocol foundry-mac-es-journal-v1 --ticket T --work-root W
  --max-output-bytes B --timeout-ms M -- <exact approved argv>`
- `stop --protocol foundry-mac-es-journal-v1 --ticket T`

The actual code passes these as argv elements, not shell text. `reserve`/`stop` use
the fixed control environment; `run` uses the exact approved plan environment and
input-root cwd. The intent digest is SHA-256 of UTF-8 `JSON.stringify` of
`[plan.argv, sortedEnvironmentEntries, inputRoot, workRoot, uid, gid, maxOutputBytes,
timeoutMs]`; environment keys sort by JavaScript `<`/`>`, independent of locale.
The supervisor must recompute this from actual launch inputs before execution.

RESERVE must exclusively create/fsync the journal and directory and may not spawn
the workload. After verifying an ARMED-only journal, the worker durably records its
payload digest before RUN. RUN must consume its ticket exactly once, own suspended
spawn, obtain kernel identity before allowing execution, durably record LAUNCH,
then resume. Caller-supplied PIDs or post-hoc account scans are not launch binding.
STOP must close the ticket permanently, including queued/late RUNs, and request
termination of known identities without PID-reuse races. Proxy close/exit does not
attest workload termination. Errors never cause replay, adoption or fresh tickets.

The worker's synchronous fence currently fences **client invocation**, not an
asynchronous privileged supervisor's later resume. Before any future activation,
the supervisor needs its own real current-authority/lease/cancel fence, bounded
request lifetime and disconnect/death handling. This is not implemented by the C
observer or invented from local configuration. Native preflight Xcode controls
currently also occur outside this observer's launch interval. Both are open gates.

## Authenticated journal wire format

File: `<evidenceRoot>/<ticket>.jsonl`. Maximum 8 MiB, 32,768 records, 16,384 text
characters per envelope, 8,192 payload bytes; at most 4,096 lineage identities.
These are reader limits, not hard native process/resource quotas. Journals are never
rotated, truncated, replaced or deleted by this package.

Each newline-terminated JSON envelope has exactly `{payload, signature}`, canonical
base64 strings. The Ed25519 signature covers the concatenation of UTF-8
`foundry-mac-es-journal-v1`, one NUL byte, and the exact decoded payload bytes.
The payload is a UTF-8 JSON tuple:

```
[protocol, index, previousPayloadSha256, ticket, bootUuid, sessionId,
 supervisorSha256, "process-events-only", body]
```

Index starts at zero and increments by one. Initial previous digest is 64 zeros.
Boot UUID is lower-case; session ID is a fresh 64-hex identifier for one ES client
lifetime. Every row pins both. No reconnect or subscription mutation is continuous
recovery. Digests hash payload bytes, excluding signature/envelope. Unknown body
types, including attempts to assert stronger coverage, are rejected.

Process identity is `[auditTokenWords[8], startSecondsDecimal, startMicroseconds]`.
Counters and seconds use canonical nonnegative decimal strings (uint64), never JSON
floating-point representations. Audit words are uint32. Root and authenticated
worker account fields must match the launch binding. Descendant events are replayed
across all UIDs; unexplained credential/PID identity transitions invalidate evidence.

Bodies are:

```
["armed", attemptHash, profileHash, intentHash, uid, gid, workerIdentity,
 globalBaseline, [forkBaseline, execBaseline, exitBaseline], machBaseline,
 "unmuted-fork-exec-exit"]
["launch", rootIdentity]
["event", messageVersion, globalSequence, typeSequence, machTime,
 ["fork", sourceIdentity, childIdentity]]
["event", messageVersion, globalSequence, typeSequence, machTime,
 ["exec", oldIdentity, targetIdentity]]
["event", messageVersion, globalSequence, typeSequence, machTime,
 ["exit", sourceIdentity]]
["seal", finalGlobal, [finalFork, finalExec, finalExit], finalMach,
 "ticket-consumed-no-relaunch", "fsync-file-and-directory", "process-events-only"]
```

ARMED must precede all job execution, with established counters and audited unmuted
subscriptions owned by the same continuous collector. All subscribed events,
including unrelated host processes, must be projected; otherwise global continuity
cannot be checked. The raw C event tuple matches the `event` body but is unsigned.
Converting raw events into these signed rows, serializing LAUNCH with event delivery,
and durable baseline/arm handoff are missing privileged supervisor work, not a
callback or a permissive default supplied here.

FORK enrolls children while retaining orphaned/reparented descendants. EXEC advances
one lifetime (same PID/start time) to its target audit identity; it is not EXIT.
Duplicate/reused active identities, missing transitions and events after a retired
identity fail. Global and per-event counters must increase by exactly one; Mach
time cannot regress (conservatively reject unusual delivery ordering). SEAL requires
a launched root, an empty replayed live set, matching final counters, permanent
ticket closure and the supervisor's durable-write assertion. No rows may follow it.
Reading a signed assertion cannot itself independently measure fsync or OS coverage.

The reader checks root ownership, no symlink/hardlink, private evidence permissions,
file size and metadata stability around a bounded descriptor read. It authenticates
every row and verifies the previously observed prefix, preventing truncation and
same-session prefix replacement from being silently adopted. Any ambiguous read
poisons active evidence. A signed process-only seal still cannot prove service work.

## Recovery and native release conditions

The existing permanent host attempt journal preserves these markers before RUN:

- `native:<attemptHash>:<ticket>`
- `native-intent:<intentHash>:<uid>:<gid>`
- `native-evidence:<armedPayloadDigest>`

Its pre-run record already contains local `profileHash`. Together these reconstruct
the original `MacLaunchCheckpoint`. Recovery must use those retained original bytes,
never a newly signed ARMED row, regenerated recipe, fresh ticket or latest session.
Missing/ambiguous markers retain quarantine. `reviewRecovery(checkpoint)` only reads
and verifies evidence; it never resumes, signals, reconciles authority or clears slots.
The parent owns any explicit recovery UI/integration; none was added to storage code.

Current release condition: **none**. Both native paths return unknown StopProof,
including exit zero and a valid signed process-only seal. This preserves the
`LongJobSupervisor` → `HostJournal.finish` quarantine path and prevents publication.
The collector intentionally subscribes only to NOTIFY events; it does not enforce
exec policy. Public ES provides `AUTH_EXEC` and `NOTIFY_FORK`; this implementation
does not assume an `AUTH_FORK` API. AUTH_EXEC enforcement alone cannot stop fork-only
code or prove closure of existing shared services. Invisible service escape is
rejected at the release boundary by refusing every native proof in this version.

Future release needs an independently reviewed, authorized, continuously owned
supervisor that closes both process lineage and every launchd/XPC/shared-service
work path, authenticates launch-time identity and current authority, handles
crash/drop/mute/queue gaps, durably closes all launch/relaunch capabilities, and
supplies verified terminal service evidence. No account-only scan, responsibility
token, process group, launchd label, quiet interval or configuration boolean supplies
that missing capability. Ordinary Xcode projects/build scripts must be explicitly
trusted and approved. Hostile-code isolation and hard native quotas are not claimed.

